      "content": "We're seeing customers extend their decision timelines on new tech investments, but they're still spending on tools that directly cut costs or improve productivity\u2014it's all about demonstrating immediate ROI right now, not long-term potential."
    },
    {
      "day": 220,
      "content": "We're seeing real hesitation in the market\u2014the 48.5 PMI confirms what our sales team is experiencing daily. Technology projects that would normally close in 60 days are now stretching to 120, and every vendor conversation starts with \"prove the cost savings\" rather than \"show us the roadmap.\" We're pivoting our pitch from transformation to immediate payback."
    },
    {
      "day": 220,
      "content": "Tech spending is in limbo right now\u2014the 48.5 PMI suggests companies aren't panicking, but they're absolutely not confident enough to bet big on new software platforms or cloud migration projects. We're seeing IT budgets shift toward maintenance and cost optimization rather than innovation, which is a meaningful tell about where executives' heads are at."
    },
    {
      "day": 220,
      "content": "We're seeing decision-makers shift from \"nice-to-have\" to \"must-have\" conversations\u2014at 48.5 PMI, companies aren't pulling back dramatically, but they're ruthlessly prioritizing which tools actually move the needle on their bottom line. Our pitch has to prove ROI in weeks, not months."
    },
    {
      "day": 220,
      "content": "With the PMI sitting right at the contraction line, I'm seeing clients take a \"show me the savings\" approach to tech spending\u2014SaaS renewals are happening, but new platform investments are getting delayed until there's more clarity. The hesitation is real, but it's not panic, which tells me businesses are bracing for uncertainty rather than expecting a sharp downturn."
    },
    {
      "day": 220,
      "content": "We're seeing procurement teams shift from \"what can we build?\" to \"what can we optimize?\"\u2014at 48.5 PMI, tech budgets aren't frozen, but they're definitely more scrutinized, so our pitch has evolved from feature-rich to cost-per-outcome. The companies moving fastest right now are the ones treating SaaS investments as operational levers, not growth bets."
    },
    {
      "day": 220,
